//kage:unit pixels

package main

// The one shape shader of gift.
//
// Every drawing operation of the display list is a rounded rectangle, filled
// or stroked, so exactly one shader and one draw call serve all of them. The
// project plan, section 8, requires analytic geometry in a shared shape
// shader and forbids an offscreen image per widget; this is that shader.
//
// Everything the shape needs travels in the vertex attributes, not in
// uniforms: uniforms are a map[string]any in Ebitengine's API and would
// allocate once per operation, which the project plan, section 11, does not
// allow in the frame path.
//
//	src0Pos  position inside the rectangle, 0..size, origin top left
//	color    premultiplied fill or stroke colour
//	custom.x half width
//	custom.y half height
//	custom.z corner radius, already clamped by the CPU side
//	custom.w stroke width; zero means fill, negative means shadow
//
// # Why a shadow is in here and not in a shader of its own
//
// Because it fits. A shadow needs a half extent, a corner radius and a blur,
// which is four numbers, and a vertex has exactly four custom attributes. The
// stroke slot carries the negated standard deviation of the Gaussian, so a
// shadow is a sign bit rather than a material.
//
// That is not a micro optimisation, it is the whole draw call budget. A
// separate shader is a separate material, and the project plan, section 11,
// forbids reordering transparent content to merge materials back together — so
// a panel with a shadow, a background and a border would have cost three draw
// calls instead of sharing one run with everything else on screen. See
// Renderer.material.
//
// # No screen space derivatives
//
// All five geometric values above arrive pre-scaled to device pixels. The CPU
// side knows the local-to-device scale of the transform — see the renderer's
// deviceScale — and bakes it into the half extents, the radius, the stroke
// width and the local position before it writes the vertex. The distance
// field below is therefore already measured in device pixels, so the width of
// the antialiasing band is the constant 1 and this shader needs no fwidth,
// dfdx or dfdy at all.
//
// That is deliberate, but the original reason for it turned out to be wrong
// and is recorded here so nobody reinstates it. The project plan once named
// derivatives as the highest-variance unknown of the project, on the theory
// that Ebitengine might fall back to GLSL ES 1.00 on a Raspberry Pi 4, where
// dfdx and dfdy need the OES_standard_derivatives extension. Ebitengine 2.10.1
// has no ES 1.00 path at all: internal/shaderir/glsl emits either #version 150
// or #version 300 es, and nothing else. Derivatives are core in GLSL ES 3.00.
//
// The rewrite is kept on its own merits, which are real. Coverage in device
// pixels fixed a latent bug in the vertex padding, which used to pad one
// *local* unit and therefore clipped the outer half of every antialiased edge
// under a downscale. It also removed the artefacts fwidth produced at the
// kinks of the distance field — the corner diagonals and the centre line of a
// stroke band — where a finite difference across a 2x2 fragment quad straddles
// a fold and blurs pixels that are genuinely fully covered. And it makes the
// output independent of how a driver lays out those quads.
//
// The actual Pi risk is failing to obtain an ES 3.0 context at all, which
// would break the whole renderer rather than just rounded corners, and which
// no shader rewrite can help with.

// sdRoundBox is the signed distance from p to a rounded box centred at the
// origin with half extents b and corner radius r. Negative inside.
func sdRoundBox(p vec2, b vec2, r float) float {
	q := abs(p) - b + vec2(r)
	return length(max(q, vec2(0))) + min(max(q.x, q.y), 0.0) - r
}

// phiApprox is the logistic approximation of the normal cumulative
// distribution, 1/(1+exp(-(1.5976x + 0.070565x^3))), whose maximum absolute
// error is about 1.4e-4 — well under one step of an eight bit alpha channel.
// x is clamped at six sigma so that the cube cannot overflow a float, which
// costs nothing because the geometry stops at three.
func phiApprox(x float) float {
	c := clamp(x, -6.0, 6.0)
	t := 1.5976*c + 0.070565*c*c*c
	return 1.0 / (1.0 + exp(-t))
}

// shadowCoverage is the coverage of a Gaussian blurred rounded box at the
// local point p, with half extents half, corner radius radius and standard
// deviation sigma.
//
// # What is exact and what is not
//
// The exact answer is the 2D convolution of the indicator function of the
// rounded box with a Gaussian. Two special cases of it have closed forms, and
// this function is a blend of both.
//
//   - Along a straight edge the convolution separates and collapses to the
//     Gaussian cumulative distribution of the perpendicular distance, which
//     is what the signed distance field term gives. Away from the corners
//     that term is exact, not an approximation. Measured maximum error at the
//     middle of an edge: 3e-4 for a shape several sigma across.
//   - At the corner of a *sharp* rectangle the convolution separates the
//     other way, into the product of two independent one dimensional answers,
//     one per axis. That term is exact for radius zero, at every point.
//
// Neither is exact at the corner of a rounded box, and nothing derivative
// free is. The blend takes the second term where it is right — a radius small
// compared to sigma — and the first where it is right, and the weight is a
// per shape constant, so a fragment pays one compare when the radius wins.
//
// # The error, measured
//
// Numbers from a reference convolution of the very same sdRoundBox, the x
// integral in closed form and the y integral over 8000 samples, taken as the
// maximum absolute coverage error over the whole corner quadrant:
//
//	half      radius  sigma   before   after
//	(40,15)   6       8       0.194    0.106
//	(40,15)   0       8       0.250    0.030
//	(50,50)   0       2       0.250    0.000
//	(50,50)   4       16      0.242    0.020
//	(50,50)   8       8       0.170    0.133
//	(50,50)   12      8       0.132    0.132
//	(50,50)   18      8       0.093    0.093
//	(50,50)   18      2       0.022    0.022
//	(30,30)   15      4       0.055    0.055
//
// So the honest statement, which replaces the "roughly two per cent" this
// comment used to claim and which was wrong by an order of magnitude: the
// corner error is up to 0.13 of the shadow alpha, it grows with sigma over
// radius, and it is largest when the two are comparable. A sharp cornered
// shadow — the default, since CornerRadius is zero unless asked for — is now
// essentially exact instead of being the worst case. The error is confined to
// the corner quadrants, is monotone and never produces a ring or a step.
//
// The alternatives remain a loop of erf evaluations per fragment, or a
// texture. Every analytic box shadow in every browser makes some version of
// this trade.
func shadowCoverage(p vec2, half vec2, radius float, sigma float) float {
	sdf := phiApprox(-sdRoundBox(p, half, radius) / sigma)
	// A per shape constant: the shader recomputes it rather than receiving it
	// because all four custom vertex slots are spoken for, and a divide plus
	// a smoothstep is cheaper than a fifth attribute would be anyway.
	w := 1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, 1.5, radius/sigma)
	if w <= 0.0 {
		return sdf
	}
	d := abs(p) - half
	prod := phiApprox(-d.x/sigma) * phiApprox(-d.y/sigma)
	return sdf + (prod-sdf)*w
}

func Fragment(dstPos vec4, src0Pos vec2, color vec4, custom vec4) vec4 {
	half := custom.xy
	radius := custom.z
	stroke := custom.w

	// A negative stroke slot is a shadow and its magnitude is sigma, already
	// in device pixels like everything else here. The quad the CPU side
	// emitted is the shape grown by three sigma, so the falloff is contained.
	if stroke < 0.0 {
		p := src0Pos - half
		return color * shadowCoverage(p, half, radius, -stroke)
	}

	// A plain axis aligned fill needs no distance field at all. The quad the
	// CPU side emitted is already exactly the rectangle, clipped, so shading
	// it uniformly is both cheaper and free of the seams an antialiased edge
	// would leave between two adjacent fills.
	if radius <= 0.0 {
		if stroke <= 0.0 {
			return color
		}
	}

	// The position arrives as 0..size so that no vertex attribute is ever
	// negative; the distance field wants it centred.
	p := src0Pos - half

	d := sdRoundBox(p, half, radius)

	if stroke > 0.0 {
		// The stroke lies inside the bounds: the band runs from -stroke to
		// 0, so recentring the distance on -stroke/2 turns it into a fill of
		// a band of that width. See the project plan, section 8.
		d = abs(d+stroke*0.5) - stroke*0.5
	}

	// d is in device pixels, so the coverage ramp is one pixel wide and
	// centred on the boundary. Scaling the whole vec4, colour and alpha
	// together, is what keeps the result premultiplied.
	return color * clamp(0.5-d, 0.0, 1.0)
}
